   "ageDays": 0.04,
   "summary": "John Inalegwu Otache was released last Sunday, October 18, after being kidnapped in north-central Nigeria. According to police authorities in Benue, Father Otache was rescued by security forces, during which other kidnapped people were released and taken to University Hospital of Otukpo. A senior advisor to the Nigerian President rejected claims that Christians were being targeted in violence in comments to Premier Christian News this March.",
   "summary_zh": "John Inalegwu Otache于去年10月18日星期日获释，此前他在尼日利亚中北部遭到绑架。据贝努埃州警方称，Otache神父由安全部队营救，期间另有被绑架人员获救，并被送往奥图克波大学医院。尼日利亚总统的高级顾问今年3月接受Premier Christian News采访时，驳斥了有关基督徒成为暴力袭击目标的说法。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Suggestions Top Stories JD Vance leads prayer at campaign rally after woma... Africa edges past Europe in global Catholic popula... Report: CofE 'needs to reduce dioceses to avoid fi... Christian leaders challenge Bridget Phillipson ove... Most Read Popular Videos World News Fri Oct 23 2026 by Joseph Terry A Nigerian priest has been rescued after being held hostage for five days as Christian persecution continues in Nigeria. His release has been confirmed by the Fides Agency, an arm of the Pontifical Mission Societies. Fr. John Inalegwu Otache was released last Sunday, October 18, after being kidnapped in north-central Nigeria. “With hearts overflowing with joy and gratitude to God, we announce the release of our priest, Reverend Father John Inalegwu Otache”, the Diocese of Otukpo announced. According to police authorities in Benue, Father Otache was rescued by security forces, during which other kidnapped people were released and taken to University Hospital of Otukpo. Fides Agency noted that local sources reported that citizen vigilante groups were involved in the rescue operation. Priests are being increasingly targeted in Nigeria, with the diocese of Otukpo suffering another incident when a seminarian escaped his captors after being held for more than two weeks. Cardinal John Onaiyekan, Archbishop Emeritus of Abuja, urged the Nigerian government to take measures to put an end to kidnappings. A senior advisor to the Nigerian President rejected claims that Christians were being targeted in violence in comments to Premier Christian News this March. However, a US House of Representatives report from February described Nigeria as the most dangerous place in the world to practice the Christian faith. Lydia Nwankwo, a Christian volunteer with the Nigerian Red Cross, was shot dead at the start of October alongside her sister Victoria. And in June, 28 Christians were targeted and killed by Fulani herdsmen, with attackers targeting and hunting down Christians, including the pastor of the Church of Christ in Nations, Rev Markus Nyam. Persecution watchdog Open Doors ranks Nigeria seventh on their World Watch List and reported that nearly 3,500 Christians were killed in Nigeria last year. More than 7 in 10 Christians killed for their faith across the world in 2025 were killed in Nigeria. Rapper Nicki Minaj recently spoke out about the oppression of Christians in Nigeria, calling on celebrities and influencers to join her call to highlight Christian persecution in the country. Your gift today ensures that Premier Christian News can press forward in strength, reaching more people with biblical truth and shaping the future of Christian thought. It’s more than just a donation—it’s an investment in renewed minds and transformed lives. Without continued support, the ability to create and distribute resources that strengthen faith and equip the church will be compromised. But with your help, we can grow deeper, stand firmer, and shine brighter in the culture. Continue the conversation on our Facebook page Article by Joseph Terry Donate Donate Unsupported Browser Sorry, your web browser is not capable of supporting the features required by this website. We advise you upgrade your web browser before continuing to use this website. Please contact your system administrator if you require assistance upgrading or changing your web browser. We will not notify you again about this until you next restart your browser",
