   "title": "Three issues defining the 2026 US midterm elections",
   "url": "https://www.rappler.com/world/us-canada/issues-defining-2026-us-midterm-elections/",
   "domain": "rappler.com",
   "source": "rappler.com",
   "published": "Wed, 21 Oct 2026 08:13:02 +0800",
   "_dt": 1792541582.0,
   "ageDays": 2.73,
   "summary": "The upcoming midterm elections on November 3 are influenced by President Trump's low popularity, voter concerns about the cost of living, and immigration issues. WASHINGTON, USA – US President Donald Trump’s record-low popularity, voter frustration over the cost of living and a fierce immigration fight are shaping the November 3 midterm elections, which will determine whether Republicans keep control of Congress or Democrats gain the power to challenge his administration.",
   "summary_zh": "即将于11月3日举行的中期选举受到特朗普总统低支持率、选民对生活成本的担忧以及移民问题的影响。华盛顿，美国——美国总统唐纳德·特朗普创纪录的低支持率、选民对生活成本的挫败感以及激烈的移民争斗，正在塑造11月3日的中期选举，这次选举将决定共和党能否继续控制国会，或民主党能否获得权力来挑战他的政府。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Already have Rappler+? Sign in to listen to groundbreaking journalism. The upcoming midterm elections on November 3 are influenced by President Trump's low popularity, voter concerns about the cost of living, and immigration issues. Voter frustration over affordability is a key issue, with rising costs in healthcare, gas, and housing affecting how people will vote, while Democrats aim to hold Trump accountable for broken promises. Immigration remains a contentious topic, with significant drops in migrant apprehensions and political backlash against immigration policies, leading to conflicts between Democrats and Republicans over funding and enforcement. This is AI-generated. Read the article for full context. Report any errors . WASHINGTON, USA – US President Donald Trump’s record-low popularity, voter frustration over the cost of living and a fierce immigration fight are shaping the November 3 midterm elections, which will determine whether Republicans keep control of Congress or Democrats gain the power to challenge his administration. Republicans hold narrow majorities in the House of Representatives and Senate. Democrats need a net gain of four seats to take control of each chamber. The outcome will hinge on a relatively small number of competitive races, but the campaigns are being shaped nationally by Trump himself and voter concerns over affordability and immigration. Democrats have said Trump has broken his campaign promises to lower costs and end wars. They have vowed to tackle an affordability agenda to reduce Americans’ cost of living and hold the administration accountable for alleged corruption if voters give them control of either chamber. Republicans’ top legislative achievement was a tax cut extension that slashed roughly $1 trillion in spending for Medicaid, the government health insurance program for low-income Americans, and other health programs. Democrats last year forced a government shutdown over a failed effort to extend expiring health subsidies that benefited 24 million Americans. Gas prices have spiked since the US and Israel launched military strikes on Iran in February. The national average price of gas is $4.28 — $1.20 higher than it was a year ago, according to AAA. The average cost of diesel is $6.32 — a $2.70 increase from a year earlier. Grocery costs are up 1.8% over the past year, according to US Bureau of Labor Statistics data on consumer prices. Federal Housing Finance Agency data show home prices rose 2.1% over the past year. Congress passed a bipartisan housing bill earlier this year. It became law without Trump’s signature. Preliminary data from the Department of Homeland Security showed a 95% drop in migrants apprehended at the southwest border compared to former president Joe Biden’s administration. The agency said there have been no Border Patrol releases the entire fiscal year for the first time ever. But fatal encounters with federal immigration agents have occurred in Maine, Minnesota and Texas. The backlash to the deaths of two US citizens in Minnesota pulled many Democrats in competitive primaries to the left this year, with some candidates calling for the impeachment of then-Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem and others saying Immigration and Customs Enforcement should be abolished. Democrats and Republicans sparred over immigration funding this year in a stalemate that forced Republicans to unilaterally pass a $70 billion bill to fund ICE and the Border Patrol for the remainder of Trump’s presidency. Republicans counter Democrats’ criticism by saying Democrats support open borders. Federal immigration officials also raided the Washington home of Democratic US Representative Ilhan Omar of Minnesota this month, drawing widespread condemnation from Democrats. A person familiar with the case against Omar told Reuters one potential aim of the investigation could be to strip her of US citizenship and deport her. Artificial intelligence and data centers, cryptocurrency, abortion and Israel could also influence voters in individual races. In Ohio’s Senate race, for example, Democratic nominee Sherrod Brown has labeled Republican Senator Jon Husted the face of data centers, and the crypto super PAC Defend American Jobs has spent more than $46 million to reelect Husted over Brown, a former US senator who chaired the chamber’s Banking Committee. In Maine’s Senate race, Democratic nominee Troy Jackson is hoping voters don’t forget that Republican Senator Susan Collins helped confirm Brett Kavanaugh to the Supreme Court, which later overturned the federal right to abortion. Collins is on the election ballot for the first time since the high court made abortion a state issue rather than a federal right. In Michigan, Republican nominee Mike Rogers has indicated he would accept help from a pro-Israel super PAC that spent more than $30 million backing Democratic US Representative Haley Stevens in her unsuccessful primary against Abdul El-Sayed, who now faces Rogers in the general election. The group has already spent more than $280,000 on mailers against El-Sayed. Upgrade to Rappler+ for exclusive content and unlimited access",
