   "platform": "x",
   "title": "Beijing has a new rule for the South China Sea: ask permission.",
   "url": "https://x.com/ShadowsOverPH/status/2112659577959576022",
   "snippet": "Beijing has a new rule for the South China Sea: ask permission.\n\nOn Tuesday, China's military said the USS Charleston sailed near Sabina Shoal \"without approval.\"\n\nIts embassy says Manila resupplied its own troops at Ayungin \"without China's consent.\"\n\nAnd the same day, near Taiwan-held Pratas, a China Coast Guard ship told Taiwan's crew the \"Republic of China does not exist.\"\n\nThree places, one message: you're only here if we allow it.\n\nHere's Beijing's problem. Sabina sits about 75 nautical miles off Palawan, inside the Philippines' EEZ. Warships don't need anyone's approval to sail there. That isn't an American talking point. It's the law of the sea China itself ratified, and China's navy uses that same freedom in other countries' EEZs, including off Alaska.\n\nLook at where the Charleston went: the shoal where a China Coast Guard ship and a Philippine vessel collided last month. Each side blames the other. The transit wasn't a request. It was an answer.\n\nAccept \"approval\" once, and every patrol, every resupply, every fishing trip becomes a request. That's the real fight in the South China Sea. Not rocks. A permission slip.",
   "body": "Beijing has a new rule for the South China Sea: ask permission.\n\nOn Tuesday, China's military said the USS Charleston sailed near Sabina Shoal \"without approval.\"\n\nIts embassy says Manila resupplied its own troops at Ayungin \"without China's consent.\"\n\nAnd the same day, near Taiwan-held Pratas, a China Coast Guard ship told Taiwan's crew the \"Republic of China does not exist.\"\n\nThree places, one message: you're only here if we allow it.\n\nHere's Beijing's problem. Sabina sits about 75 nautical miles off Palawan, inside the Philippines' EEZ. Warships don't need anyone's approval to sail there. That isn't an American talking point. It's the law of the sea China itself ratified, and China's navy uses that same freedom in other countries' EEZs, including off Alaska.\n\nLook at where the Charleston went: the shoal where a China Coast Guard ship and a Philippine vessel collided last month. Each side blames the other. The transit wasn't a request. It was an answer.\n\nAccept \"approval\" once, and every patrol, every resupply, every fishing trip becomes a request. That's the real fight in the South China Sea. Not rocks. A permission slip.",
